  "query_preview": "..(Sunday night through Friday)\nIssued at 345 AM EDT SAT SEP 12 2026\n\nAt the start of the long term period, a midlevel ridge will be\ncentered over the Southern Plains, with midlevel troughing over\nthe Great Lakes region. Eastern Kentucky will initially find\nitself between these two features, with relatively zonal flow\naloft; however, by Monday, ridging is expected to push eastward\nand amplify. Meanwhile, at the surface, a cold front will\ninitially be located near the Ohio River. Throughout Monday, this\nfront will sag southward, ushering in cooler and drier air as\nwinds shift to become more northerly to northeasterly. This will\nkeep highs closer to average in the northern forecast area, likely\nin the low 80s, with warmer temperatures farther south near the TN\nline. Even so, rain chances with this front look to be relatively\nlow, and any mentionable PoPs remain confined to the northern\nportions of the forecast area. \n\nFollowing this frontal passage, there is high confidence an\nanomalously strong ridge will build into the area and linger\nthrough at least midweek, with LREF mean 500 mb heights peaking\nWednesday at 599 dam (representing the 100th percentile compared\nto climatology!). This will suppress convection and keep\ntemperatures unseasonably hot, more characteristic of the middle\nof summer than mid September. Highs look to peak Wednesday,\ngenerally in the low 90s across the forecast area. Also, given\nincreasingly southwesterly winds around a surface high over the\nMid Atlantic, dewpoints will quickly rebound, rising into the\nupper 60s to perhaps low 70s on Tuesday and Wednesday.\nAccordingly, although lows will be in the upper 50s to low 60s on\nMonday night, they look to trend warmer into the midweek, with the\ncaveat that clear skies and diminishing winds overnight will\npromote ridge valley temperature splits that should keep valleys a\nfew degrees cooler than NBM output would suggest. Cooling\novernight will be accompanied by typical valley fog formation,\nparticularly within mainstem river valleys. \n\nModel spread increases significantly from Thursday onwards as\nshortwave energy moving through the base of a digging trough will\nattempt to suppress our pesky ridge. There is virtually no model\nagreement regarding how quickly the ridge begins to weaken, with\nsome deterministic guidance showing the forecast area near the\nbase of a trough by the end of the period, while other guidance\nkeeps the ridge around seemingly indefinitely. Still, rain chances\nare favored to return near the end of the period as another cold\nfront approaches.",
